    "title": "The Texas Cattle Drives",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "After the Civil War, Texas ranchers sought markets where cattle could command higher prices. Many herds consisted of longhorns, animals well suited to traveling across dry country. The Chisholm Trail carried cattle north through Indian Territory toward Kansas. During the war, many Texas cattle had remained on the range while the people who normally tended them were away. In northern cities, meanwhile, demand for beef created an opportunity for ranchers willing to move their animals over great distances. The difference between prices at home and prices at the destination had to cover wages, supplies and losses along the way. Longhorns could survive on relatively sparse grass, but even these hardy animals needed regular access to water. A successful drive therefore depended on the condition of the land as much as on the skill of the riders. The trail was not a paved road but a broad corridor that changed according to weather and grazing conditions.\n\nAbilene became an important Kansas railhead in 1867. Joseph McCoy helped develop the cattle shipping business there. A trail boss directed the crew and made decisions about the route. McCoy recognized that a railroad connection could bring western livestock and eastern buyers together in one location. He arranged facilities where cattle could be held before they were loaded onto trains. The journey from a ranch to a shipping point often occupied several weeks or months. A herd might contain animals belonging to more than one owner, making careful record keeping important. The boss had to judge whether to continue moving or stop early when the animals appeared exhausted. A shorter route was not always the best choice if it offered little grass or water.\n\nThe remuda was the collection of spare riding horses used by the cowboys. A chuck wagon carried cooking equipment and provisions for the crew. Riders at the front helped guide the herd, while riders at the rear worked in the dust. A wrangler looked after the spare horses, which allowed the other riders to change mounts during a long day. The cook often traveled ahead of the herd so a meal could be prepared before the riders arrived. Beans, biscuits and coffee were practical foods because supplies had to survive the journey without modern refrigeration. The riders on the sides of the herd prevented animals from wandering away into nearby country. Working behind the cattle was particularly uncomfortable when the ground was dry. At night, riders took turns watching the animals while the rest of the crew slept.\n\nAt river crossings, deep water and strong currents could scatter cattle. A sudden noise could trigger a stampede, especially at night. Cowboys sometimes sang to help keep resting cattle calm. Before entering a river, experienced riders looked for a place where the banks were not too steep. An animal that turned back could cause others to follow, creating confusion among the herd. During a stampede, the crew might attempt to guide the running animals into a broad circle until they slowed. Lightning, unfamiliar smells and sudden movements could disturb cattle that had been resting peacefully. Wet clothing and muddy ground made ordinary tasks more difficult after a storm. Losing even a few animals could reduce the profit earned by the owners.\n\nBrands helped ranchers identify ownership of animals. The Western Trail later carried herds toward places such as Dodge City. Railroads made it possible to ship cattle onward to distant markets. At the destination, buyers examined the condition of the animals before agreeing on a price. Cattle that had lost too much weight during the trip might bring less money than expected. A railroad journey could move animals much farther in a shorter period than a traditional drive. Towns near shipping points developed hotels, stores and businesses serving both ranchers and railroad workers. Not everyone welcomed the arrival of large herds, especially farmers concerned about damage to crops. Disputes over grazing and disease helped change the routes used by cattle crews.\n\nBarbed wire fences increasingly restricted open-range travel. Quarantine rules also restricted cattle movement because of disease concerns. The great era of long-distance drives declined as rail lines expanded and land became fenced. As settlement increased, ranchers began dividing land that had once been crossed without many physical barriers. Fences made it easier to control breeding and keep animals on particular properties. New rail connections closer to Texas ranches reduced the need to walk cattle all the way to Kansas. The work of raising livestock continued even as the famous long trails became less important. Cowboys still needed to understand animal behavior, weather and the use of horses. The cattle drive remains a memorable example of how transportation and markets can reshape an entire occupation.",
