    "id": "original-04",
    "title": "The Secret Lives of Octopuses",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "Octopuses are mollusks belonging to a group called cephalopods. An octopus has eight arms rather than the ten appendages typical of squid. Suckers along the arms help grasp objects and detect chemicals. The name cephalopod refers to a group that also includes cuttlefish and nautiluses. Unlike a fish, an octopus has no backbone supporting its body. Its arms surround the mouth and can bend in many directions without ordinary joints. The suckers can attach firmly to surfaces and release again when the animal moves. Chemical information collected by the suckers helps an octopus investigate objects without relying entirely on vision. These abilities make the arms useful both for movement and for exploring unfamiliar surroundings.\n\nA hard beak helps an octopus break apart food. Most octopuses have no rigid external shell. A soft body allows an octopus to squeeze through narrow openings. The beak is located where the arms meet, rather than at the end of a long snout. It can cut through tough food that would be difficult to swallow whole. An octopus may enter a small opening as long as its relatively rigid structures can fit through it. This flexibility offers protection from larger predators that cannot follow into a narrow shelter. Muscles throughout the body provide support and allow the animal to change shape. A rocky habitat with many hiding places can therefore be especially useful.\n\nThree hearts circulate blood through the body and gills. Hemocyanin contains copper and helps carry oxygen in the blood. Chromatophores are pigment-containing structures involved in color changes. Two of the hearts send blood toward the gills, while the remaining heart supplies the rest of the body. Gills allow oxygen dissolved in seawater to enter the circulatory system. The copper-containing oxygen carrier gives oxygenated octopus blood a bluish appearance. Color changes in the skin can occur remarkably quickly as muscles alter the appearance of pigment structures. Other structures reflect light and contribute to the animal's visual appearance. Camouflage depends on both the pattern of the surroundings and the octopus's ability to respond to them.\n\nSome octopuses change skin texture as well as color. An octopus can expel water through a siphon to move rapidly. Many species release ink to distract predators. A smooth animal resting against a rough rock might be noticeable even if its color matched the rock. Some species can raise small projections on their skin to imitate an uneven surface. Water drawn into the mantle cavity can be forced outward through the siphon. The resulting jet moves the animal in the opposite direction from the escaping water. Jet propulsion is useful for rapid escape, while crawling may be better for quiet exploration. Ink can obscure a predator's view long enough for an octopus to reach shelter.\n\nCrabs and other small marine animals are common prey. Many octopuses shelter in rocky crevices or dens. Some have been observed opening containers to obtain food. An octopus hunting a crab must deal with a hard shell and defensive claws. Some species use their arms to hold prey while the beak and other feeding structures do their work. Empty shells near a den may reveal what an octopus has been eating. In experiments, researchers have offered food inside containers to observe how animals solve unfamiliar problems. Success in such tasks demonstrates flexible behavior rather than a simple automatic response. Different species and individuals may respond differently to the same challenge.\n\nA large portion of their neurons is located in the arms. Females of many species guard their eggs until they hatch. Octopuses live in marine environments ranging from shallow reefs to deep water. The nervous system allows the arms to perform some complicated movements with considerable local control. This arrangement is useful because eight flexible arms can encounter different objects at the same time. Many females clean and ventilate their eggs while remaining close to the nest. In numerous species, reproduction is followed by the death of the adult rather than another breeding season. Young octopuses face many predators before reaching maturity. The variety of habitats and behaviors found among octopuses makes it important not to assume that every species lives in exactly the same way.",
