    "id": "original-05",
    "title": "The Migration of Monarch Butterflies",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "Monarch butterflies are recognizable by orange wings marked with black veins. Eastern North American monarchs often overwinter in central Mexico. Many gather in high-elevation forests dominated by oyamel firs. The bright markings of a monarch are useful to observers trying to distinguish it from other butterflies. The eastern population undertakes a remarkable seasonal journey rather than remaining in the same place throughout the year. Mountains in Mexico offer a combination of elevation, shelter and temperature that differs from the surrounding lowlands. Butterflies may gather in dense clusters on branches and trunks. The forest canopy helps moderate exposure to wind and changing weather. Protecting these winter sites matters because many individuals concentrate within relatively small areas.\n\nThe cool forest conditions help conserve stored energy. Monarchs use a sun compass that is adjusted by their internal clock. The fall migration may cover thousands of miles. A butterfly must balance the energy used in flight against the energy it obtains from food. Cooler conditions can slow activity and help preserve fat reserves during the winter. The position of the sun changes throughout the day, so a compass based on sunlight must account for time. Monarchs also take advantage of favorable winds when traveling long distances. They do not need to flap continuously every moment of the journey. Stops at flowering plants allow migrating individuals to obtain additional energy.\n\nThe migratory generation can live much longer than typical summer generations. In spring, monarchs move north and reproduce along the way. Several generations complete the annual cycle across North America. Summer monarchs generally reproduce relatively soon after reaching adulthood. The generation that migrates south delays reproduction and survives through the winter. In spring, returning butterflies begin the journey north before laying eggs. Their descendants continue the movement into more northern regions. No single butterfly normally completes every stage of the entire annual cycle. This multigenerational pattern distinguishes the monarch journey from migrations in which the same adult travels back and forth each year.\n\nFemale monarchs lay eggs on milkweed plants. Caterpillars feed on milkweed leaves. Milkweed chemicals can make monarchs unpleasant to some predators. A female searches for a suitable host plant because newly hatched caterpillars cannot travel far to find food. The caterpillar grows by eating leaves and periodically shedding its outer covering. Milkweed contains defensive chemicals that some monarchs can retain in their bodies. Bright coloration may warn experienced predators that the insect is an unpleasant meal. Not every predator is deterred by these defenses, and many young monarchs fail to survive. The availability of host plants is therefore only one factor affecting population size.\n\nThe pupa is enclosed in a chrysalis. Adults drink nectar from flowering plants. Habitat loss can reduce the availability of milkweed and nectar plants. Inside the chrysalis, the insect undergoes a major transformation before emerging as an adult butterfly. A newly emerged monarch must expand and dry its wings before flying normally. Adult butterflies need nectar, which supplies sugars used for activity. A landscape containing milkweed but few flowering plants may not meet all their needs. Mowing, development and changes in land management can reduce suitable habitat. Gardens with flowers blooming at different times can help provide resources across a longer season.\n\nWestern monarchs also use overwintering sites along coastal California. Scientists use lightweight tags to learn about movement and migration. Planting suitable native flowers can support monarchs during their journeys. Western monarchs have different migration patterns, and some travel to Mexico rather than remaining along the California coast. Tagging programs depend on people recording where a butterfly was marked and where it was later found. A single recovered tag can connect two distant locations on a migration map. Large numbers of observations help scientists investigate routes and timing. Weather conditions can affect both the journey and the number of butterflies reaching winter sites. Conservation efforts must consider breeding habitat, migration corridors and overwintering forests together.",
