    "title": "The World of Honeybees",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "A honeybee colony contains a queen, workers and drones. The queen's main role is laying eggs. Worker honeybees are female. A colony functions as a community in which different individuals perform different tasks. The queen is not a ruler issuing instructions to each worker but a reproductive member of the group. Chemical signals help coordinate activities and maintain the organization of the colony. Workers may change jobs as they age, beginning with tasks inside the hive before moving to outside work. Young bees often care for developing larvae and clean cells. Older workers may guard the entrance or search for food beyond the nest.\n\nDrones are male bees whose reproductive role is mating with queens. Workers collect nectar from flowers. Pollen supplies important nutrients, including protein. Drones do not gather nectar or pollen in the same way as foraging workers. Their reproductive function helps connect the genetic history of different colonies. A worker visiting flowers collects sugary liquid that can provide energy for the colony. Pollen supplies materials needed for growth, particularly when young bees are being raised. A flower may offer nectar, pollen or both depending on its species. Bees must find enough suitable plants within flying distance to meet the changing needs of the hive.\n\nHoneybees carry pollen between flowers and can help plants reproduce. Workers produce wax from glands on their abdomens. Honeycomb cells have a hexagonal shape. Pollen grains can cling to a bee's body as it moves around a flower. Some grains are transferred when the bee visits another flower of the same plant species. This movement can allow seeds and fruits to develop after fertilization. Workers use wax to construct cells for storing food and raising young. The six-sided arrangement fits together without leaving large gaps between neighboring cells. A comb is therefore both a food storage system and a nursery.\n\nNectar is processed and its water content reduced to make honey. A waggle dance communicates information about a food source. The dance conveys direction relative to the sun and information about distance. Fresh nectar usually contains more water than finished honey. Workers handle the liquid and expose it to air, helping remove excess moisture. Stored honey provides energy when flowers are scarce or weather prevents foraging. A successful forager can return to the colony and communicate information about a promising location. Other workers interpret movements made on the comb and may fly toward the indicated area. This communication helps a colony concentrate effort on resources that would be difficult for every bee to discover independently.\n\nWorkers fan their wings to help regulate hive conditions. A swarm may leave with a queen to establish a new colony. Varroa mites are parasites that threaten honeybee health. During hot weather, workers may bring water into the hive and help evaporate it. Moving air with their wings contributes to temperature regulation around developing young. When a colony swarms, a group of bees may gather temporarily while scouts investigate possible nesting sites. The original nest can continue functioning with a replacement queen. Parasites such as Varroa mites can weaken bees and contribute to the spread of viruses. Colony health depends on nutrition, disease management and suitable environmental conditions.\n\nBeekeepers often use removable frames to inspect combs. Flower diversity can provide food across different seasons. Honeybees are important pollinators, but many other insects also pollinate plants. Removable frames allow a beekeeper to examine stored food and developing brood without destroying the entire nest. Observations can reveal whether the colony has sufficient resources or signs of disease. A field containing only one flowering crop may offer abundant food for a brief period and very little afterward. A variety of plants can help fill those seasonal gaps. Wild bees, flies, butterflies and other insects also contribute to pollination. Protecting pollinator habitat involves more than simply increasing the number of managed honeybee hives.",
