    "id": "original-07",
    "title": "The Science of Lightning",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "Thunderstorms develop when moist air rises and cools. Within a storm, collisions involving ice particles help separate electric charges. The lower part of a thundercloud often develops a negative charge. Warm moist air can become buoyant and rise when atmospheric conditions are favorable. As it rises, the air expands and cools, allowing water vapor to condense into droplets. Strong thunderstorms may contain rising and falling currents that move water and ice through different temperature zones. Collisions among particles contribute to the separation of electrical charge. The detailed distribution of charge can vary from one storm to another. A thundercloud is therefore not simply a large container filled with rain.\n\nAn electric field can develop between a cloud and the ground. Lightning is a sudden electrical discharge. Many lightning flashes occur within clouds rather than reaching the ground. Opposite charges attract, while charges of the same sign repel each other. A strong electric field can develop as separated charges accumulate. Air normally resists the movement of electrical current, but sufficiently intense fields can create conducting paths. A discharge may connect different regions of a cloud or connect a cloud with the ground. Flashes within clouds are common even when no bolt reaches a visible object below. Observers may see an entire cloud illuminate rather than a sharply defined channel.\n\nA stepped leader can advance toward the ground in stages. A return stroke produces much of the bright light seen in a cloud-to-ground flash. Lightning rapidly heats surrounding air. In a common type of cloud-to-ground flash, the leader advances along an irregular branching path. As it approaches the surface, upward discharges may develop from objects below. When a connection forms, a powerful current travels through the established channel. The bright return stroke is the part most easily noticed by an observer. More than one stroke may follow the same channel, creating the appearance of flickering. These events occur far too quickly for an unaided observer to distinguish every stage.\n\nRapid expansion of heated air produces thunder. Light travels much faster than sound. A delay between flash and thunder indicates distance from the lightning. The air surrounding a lightning channel becomes extremely hot in a very short time. Heated air expands rapidly and produces a pressure disturbance that travels outward as sound. Thunder may begin with a sharp crack and continue as a rumble because different parts of the channel lie at different distances. Light reaches an observer almost immediately compared with the arrival of sound. Counting seconds after a flash provides a rough estimate rather than an exact measurement. Wind and atmospheric conditions can also affect the way thunder is heard.\n\nAbout five seconds between flash and thunder corresponds to roughly one mile. A substantial enclosed building is a safer shelter during a thunderstorm. A fully enclosed hard-topped vehicle can also offer protection. The distance estimate is useful for understanding a storm but should not be used as a reason to delay seeking shelter. A person who hears thunder is close enough to a storm to take lightning seriously. An enclosed building with substantial construction provides better protection than an open picnic shelter. A hard-topped vehicle protects occupants through its enclosing conductive structure rather than through its rubber tires. Outdoor activities should be planned with a safe shelter in mind before storms develop. Weather forecasts and alerts can help people make decisions before dangerous conditions arrive.\n\nLightning can strike outside the area of heavy rain. Experts recommend waiting thirty minutes after the last thunder before resuming outdoor activity. Lightning detection networks help meteorologists monitor storm activity. A lightning flash can extend well beyond the portion of a cloud producing heavy rain. Blue sky overhead does not necessarily mean a nearby storm presents no danger. Waiting after the last thunder allows time for a departing storm to move farther away. Detection networks use signals from electrical discharges to estimate where flashes occur. Meteorologists combine this information with radar and other observations to understand storm development. Lightning research helps improve both scientific knowledge and practical decisions about outdoor safety.",
