    "title": "The International Space Station",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "The International Space Station is a laboratory orbiting Earth. Its first module, Zarya, was launched in 1998. The first long-duration crew arrived in November 2000. Building a laboratory in orbit required many separate launches because the completed structure was too large to carry into space at once. Zarya provided important early functions while additional modules were attached. Different components were designed and built by teams working in several countries. The arrival of a long-duration crew marked the beginning of continuous human occupation. Assembly and maintenance continued after astronauts began living aboard. The station became a place where people could conduct experiments over periods much longer than a brief spacecraft flight.\n\nThe station travels in low Earth orbit. A typical orbit takes about ninety minutes. Astronauts experience microgravity because they and the station are continuously falling around Earth. Low Earth orbit is close enough to the planet for its gravity to remain strong. The station moves sideways so rapidly that its falling path continually curves around Earth. Astronauts and loose objects follow nearly the same motion as the surrounding spacecraft. This shared motion creates the experience of floating even though gravity has not disappeared. During an orbit, the crew may pass from sunlight into darkness and back again. The rapid succession of day and night differs greatly from the daily cycle experienced on the ground.\n\nLarge solar arrays convert sunlight into electricity. Batteries provide power when the station passes through Earth's shadow. Radiators help release excess heat into space. Solar arrays contain cells that convert incoming light into electrical energy. The station needs electricity for computers, lighting, experiments and equipment that maintains breathable air. Batteries store energy for periods when Earth blocks direct sunlight. Electronic equipment and human activity generate heat that must be removed from the living spaces. Cooling systems carry heat to external radiators, which release energy as infrared radiation. Unlike an ordinary outdoor radiator on Earth, a spacecraft radiator cannot rely on surrounding air to carry heat away.\n\nLife-support systems help recycle water. Astronauts exercise to reduce muscle and bone loss. Experiments examine how plants and materials behave in microgravity. Transporting every drop of water from Earth would require considerable cargo capacity. Recycling equipment recovers water from sources such as humidity and processed waste. Air quality must also be monitored because the crew cannot simply open a window for fresh air. Without ordinary weight-bearing activity, muscles and bones can weaken over time. Exercise equipment provides resistance and other forms of physical training. Scientists study these changes to understand the challenges of longer missions beyond Earth orbit.\n\nCargo spacecraft deliver food, equipment and other supplies. Docking and berthing connect visiting spacecraft to the station. Spacewalks allow astronauts to perform some exterior maintenance. Cargo vehicles bring replacement parts as well as everyday supplies needed by the crew. Some vehicles attach using their own docking systems, while others have used robotic assistance for berthing. Connecting spacecraft requires careful coordination because both vehicles are moving rapidly around Earth. Outside work requires protective suits that supply oxygen and help control temperature. Astronauts use tethers and handrails to remain connected to the station during spacewalks. Much of the work involves inspection, repair and installation rather than exploration of distant locations.\n\nThe Canadarm2 robotic arm assists with station operations. Several nations and space agencies cooperate on the station. The station offers a platform for observing Earth and conducting research. The robotic arm can move equipment and help position astronauts during certain exterior tasks. Its ability to operate from different attachment points makes it useful on a large structure. International cooperation requires common procedures, compatible equipment and communication among ground teams. Researchers use the station to investigate combustion, fluids, biology and other subjects under unusual conditions. Cameras and instruments also observe clouds, oceans and changes on Earth's surface. The station demonstrates how a shared engineering project can support many different scientific goals.",
