    "id": "original-09",
    "title": "How Bridges Stay Standing",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "A bridge transfers loads from its deck to supports and eventually the ground. The deck is the surface that carries traffic. A beam bridge uses horizontal members supported at intervals. A bridge must carry its own weight before any vehicles or pedestrians enter it. Engineers call the weight of permanent structural components a dead load. Traffic and other changing weights contribute to live loads. The deck must distribute these forces to members capable of carrying them safely. Foundations then transfer forces into soil or rock beneath the structure. A bridge design must therefore consider the entire path followed by a load rather than only the visible roadway.\n\nCompression pushes material together. Tension pulls material apart. An arch bridge transfers much of its load through compression. A beam supported at both ends bends when a downward force is applied near its center. In a simple beam under that load, the upper region tends to be compressed while the lower region tends to be stretched. Materials respond differently to these two kinds of force. An arch uses its curved shape to direct forces toward supports at either end. Those supports must resist outward thrust as well as vertical weight. The shape of a structure can therefore be just as important as the material from which it is built.\n\nAbutments support bridge ends and resist forces from the structure. Piers provide intermediate support between the ends. A truss uses connected triangles to distribute forces. An abutment may also retain earth where a road approaches a bridge. Intermediate piers reduce the distance that individual spans must cross without support. Placing a pier in a river requires consideration of water flow and erosion around its foundation. A truss is assembled from members connected into a pattern of triangles. Triangles resist changes in shape more effectively than simple four-sided frames with freely moving joints. Engineers calculate which members are stretched and which are compressed under different loads.\n\nSuspension bridges hang their decks from cables supported by towers. Cable-stayed bridges use cables running directly from towers to the deck. Steel is useful because it can resist substantial tension. A suspension bridge uses main cables that curve between tall towers and extend toward anchorages. Smaller vertical suspenders connect the deck to the main cables. The towers transfer large forces downward, while the anchorages resist the pull of the cables. A cable-stayed bridge instead connects the deck directly to its towers through inclined cables. These designs can cross wide obstacles without placing numerous supports in the water below. The best choice depends on span length, foundation conditions, cost and construction requirements.\n\nConcrete is particularly useful in compression. Reinforced concrete contains steel reinforcement. Expansion joints accommodate changes in length as temperature changes. Steel reinforcement helps concrete structures resist forces that would otherwise produce cracks under tension. Concrete also protects embedded reinforcement when it is properly designed and maintained. Both materials change length slightly as their temperature changes. A long bridge can experience a noticeable total movement even when the change in each small section is tiny. Bearings and joints allow controlled movement without creating unnecessary stress. Drainage systems help keep water from collecting where it could accelerate deterioration.\n\nWind can create dynamic forces on a bridge. Engineers inspect bridges for deterioration such as corrosion and cracking. A safe design accounts for expected loads and provides margins against failure. Wind does not always apply a steady force because gusts and vibration can interact with the shape of a bridge. Engineers may study models to understand how a proposed structure responds to moving air. Repeated traffic loads can also cause fatigue in certain materials and connections. Inspections help identify problems before they develop into serious structural damage. Maintenance may include repairing joints, replacing protective coatings or addressing erosion near foundations. A bridge remains dependable through a combination of thoughtful design, careful construction and continued attention throughout its service life.",
