    "id": "original-10",
    "title": "The Invention of the Bicycle",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "Early human-powered two-wheeled vehicles did not all have pedals. Karl Drais demonstrated a steerable running machine in 1817. Riders pushed against the ground with their feet. The earliest bicycle-like machines appeared before roads were designed for comfortable travel on two wheels. Drais's machine had a frame connecting two wheels and a steering mechanism at the front. A rider sat on the frame and pushed alternately with the feet to gain speed. Balancing while moving required practice because there was no third wheel to provide support. The machine demonstrated that a person could travel using rolling motion without relying on an animal. Later inventors experimented with ways to keep the rider's feet off the ground while supplying power.\n\nLater velocipedes used pedals attached to the front wheel. Iron tires and rough roads gave some early cycles the nickname boneshaker. The high-wheel bicycle had a very large front wheel. Attaching pedals directly to the front wheel provided a simple mechanical connection. Each full turn of the pedals caused the driven wheel to make one revolution. Rough road surfaces transmitted jolts through rigid wheels and frames. The nickname given to some early cycles reflected the uncomfortable experience of riding them. Increasing the size of the driven wheel offered a way to travel farther with each pedal turn. This solution led to a distinctive design with a large front wheel and much smaller rear wheel.\n\nA larger driven wheel could travel farther per pedal revolution. High-wheel riders faced a risk of falling forward from a considerable height. The safety bicycle used two wheels of more similar size. The circumference of a wheel determines the distance traveled during one revolution when it rolls without slipping. A large wheel therefore increased the distance covered by each turn of directly attached pedals. However, placing the rider high above the ground made mounting and dismounting difficult. A sudden obstacle could cause the rider to pitch forward over the front wheel. Designers searched for ways to achieve useful speed without requiring such a tall machine. The safety bicycle offered a different balance between performance and stability.\n\nJohn Kemp Starley introduced the Rover safety bicycle in 1885. A chain drive transmitted pedal power to the rear wheel. Pneumatic tires contain air to cushion the ride. Starley's Rover helped establish the general arrangement familiar in modern bicycles. A chain connected a toothed wheel near the pedals to another toothed wheel at the rear hub. The relative sizes of these wheels determined how far the bicycle moved for each pedal revolution. This arrangement allowed the road wheels to remain similar in size while still providing useful gearing. Lower seating made it easier for riders to reach the ground when stopping. Improvements in tires further increased comfort and made cycling attractive to more people.\n\nJohn Boyd Dunlop helped popularize pneumatic bicycle tires in the late 1880s. Gearing allows riders to trade speed for easier pedaling. Brakes convert motion into other forms of energy, commonly heat through friction. An air-filled tire can deform around small irregularities in a road instead of transmitting every impact directly to the rider. Dunlop's work helped make pneumatic tires commercially successful for bicycles. Different gear ratios allow a cyclist to adjust effort according to hills, speed and riding conditions. A low gear makes climbing easier at the cost of traveling less distance per pedal turn. Braking systems slow the wheels by applying a force that opposes motion. Reliable brakes and tires are especially important when riding downhill or on wet surfaces.\n\nBicycles expanded personal mobility for many people. Modern bicycles may use aluminum, steel or carbon fiber frames. The bicycle remains useful for transportation, recreation and exercise. The bicycle offered many people a means of traveling independently without the expense of keeping a horse. It influenced road improvement campaigns and became useful for work as well as leisure. Modern frame designers balance strength, weight, cost and comfort when choosing materials. Some bicycles are designed for smooth roads, while others have features suited to rough trails or carrying cargo. The basic principle remains the conversion of human effort into controlled rolling motion. Its history shows how small mechanical improvements can combine to make a device widely practical.",
