    "id": "original-12",
    "title": "The Construction of the Panama Canal",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "The Isthmus of Panama separates the Caribbean Sea from the Pacific Ocean. A canal across the isthmus shortens many shipping routes. A French-led construction effort began in the 1880s. An isthmus is a narrow connection of land between larger land areas, with water on opposite sides. Before the canal, ships traveling between many Atlantic and Pacific ports had to take a much longer route around South America. A crossing through Panama promised to reduce travel distance and the time needed to deliver cargo. The terrain, however, included hills, rivers and regions of heavy tropical rainfall. Engineers had to decide whether to excavate a passage at sea level or use a system that raised vessels above it. The decision would influence nearly every part of the construction project.\n\nFerdinand de Lesseps was associated with the French project. Disease and engineering difficulties undermined the French effort. The United States took over construction in 1904. The French effort followed the success of the Suez Canal, but Panama presented very different environmental conditions. Heavy rain complicated excavation and made it difficult to keep steep slopes stable. Workers also faced diseases whose transmission was not yet adequately controlled. Financial difficulties contributed to the collapse of the original French enterprise. When the United States began its construction effort, it inherited equipment and experience from earlier work. The new project eventually adopted a lock-based design rather than attempting to cut the entire route down to sea level.\n\nMosquito control helped reduce yellow fever and malaria. William Gorgas led important public health work. The canal uses locks to raise and lower ships. Mosquitoes transmit both yellow fever and malaria, although different species and organisms are involved. Public health workers reduced breeding sites by draining standing water and applying other control measures. Screens helped prevent mosquitoes from reaching people inside buildings. These efforts made the construction area safer for workers and their families. A lock canal required large chambers with gates capable of holding back water. Ships would be lifted or lowered by changing the water level inside those chambers rather than by using a mechanical crane.\n\nGatun Dam helped create Gatun Lake. The lake forms a major part of the canal route. The Culebra Cut required excavation through difficult terrain. The dam across the Chagres River created a large artificial lake above sea level. Ships could travel across this body of water instead of passing through an excavated trench for the entire route. The lake also provided water needed for the operation of the locks. Cutting through the continental divide remained one of the most demanding engineering tasks. Excavated material had to be loaded onto trains and carried away to disposal areas. Rain and unstable slopes repeatedly added new material to places that workers had already cleared.\n\nLandslides complicated excavation work. The canal officially opened in August 1914. Ships move through lock chambers as water levels change. Landslides sometimes interrupted progress and required additional excavation beyond the original estimates. Steam shovels and rail equipment helped remove enormous quantities of rock and soil. At a lock, gates close behind a vessel before water is admitted or released. Once the chamber reaches the level of the next section, another gate can open. This sequence allows ships to move between the ocean and the elevated lake. The canal's opening in 1914 represented the completion of a project involving engineering, transportation and public health on a remarkable scale.\n\nFresh water is important to canal operations. Panama assumed full control of the canal at the end of 1999. An expanded set of locks opened in 2016 to accommodate larger vessels. Rainfall and water management remain important because lock operations depend on a supply of fresh water. The canal is also connected with water resources used by communities in Panama. Changes in ship design eventually created demand for larger lock chambers. The expansion opened in 2016 provided a route for many vessels that could not fit through the original locks. The older and newer facilities illustrate how infrastructure can be adapted as transportation needs change. The canal remains an example of how geography and engineering influence international trade.",
