    "title": "The Formation of Caves",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "Many solution caves develop in limestone, a rock rich in calcium carbonate. Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from air and soil. Dissolved carbon dioxide forms weak carbonic acid in water. Limestone commonly forms from material containing calcium carbonate, including the remains of marine organisms. Layers of this rock may later be raised above sea level by geological processes. Water entering the ground encounters gases produced by organisms and decaying material in soil. Carbon dioxide dissolves into the water and participates in chemical reactions that make it mildly acidic. The resulting water is not comparable in strength to concentrated industrial acids. Over long periods, however, repeated contact can remove substantial quantities of soluble rock.\n\nWeakly acidic water can dissolve limestone over long periods. Water follows cracks and fractures in rock. As openings enlarge, underground passages can develop. Small cracks provide pathways where water can move through otherwise solid rock. Dissolution gradually widens some of these pathways and allows more water to pass through them. A developing underground drainage system may carry water away from the surface. Changes in the water table can influence which passages remain flooded and which become filled with air. Some caves contain several levels that reflect different stages of landscape development. The shape of a cave therefore records aspects of the movement of water through the surrounding rock.\n\nKarst is a landscape shaped largely by dissolution of soluble rock. Sinkholes can form where material dissolves or collapses beneath the surface. Water dripping from a cave ceiling can deposit calcium carbonate. Karst landscapes may contain springs, disappearing streams and depressions in addition to caves. A stream that sinks underground may reappear at a spring some distance away. Surface depressions can develop gradually or form suddenly when an underground opening collapses. Buildings and roads in karst areas require careful investigation of the ground beneath them. Water entering an underground passage may carry dissolved minerals from the rock above. When conditions change, some of those minerals can be deposited again inside the cave.\n\nA stalactite hangs from the ceiling. A stalagmite grows upward from the floor. When these formations join, they can create a column. A drop hanging from a ceiling can lose carbon dioxide to the surrounding cave air. This change may cause calcium carbonate to precipitate and remain behind as a tiny deposit. Repeated dripping can gradually extend a formation downward from the ceiling. Drops reaching the floor may leave additional deposits that build upward. The shapes of these formations depend on the supply of water, chemistry and conditions within the cave. A column can form when upward and downward growth eventually meets.\n\nFlowstone forms as mineral-bearing water flows over surfaces. Cave formations generally grow slowly and can be damaged by touching. Some caves contain animals adapted to darkness. Water flowing in a thin sheet can leave mineral deposits over walls and sloping surfaces. Such deposits may resemble frozen waterfalls even though they formed through chemical processes. Oils and dirt from hands can alter delicate surfaces, and broken formations may take extremely long periods to recover. Animals living permanently in darkness may have reduced eyes or pigmentation. Food entering a cave from the surface can support organisms that cannot rely on sunlight for energy. The underground environment is connected to the surrounding landscape even when its passages are hidden from view.\n\nLava tubes form by a different process involving flowing molten rock. Cave maps help explorers understand passage relationships. Protecting surface water quality can help protect underground cave ecosystems. A lava tube develops when the surface of a moving lava flow cools while molten material continues beneath it. If the interior lava drains away, an empty passage may remain without any limestone dissolution. Explorers use measurements of direction, distance and elevation to create maps of underground routes. Maps can help scientists understand drainage and assist with conservation planning. Pollution entering a sinkhole may travel rapidly through connected passages with little natural filtration. Protecting caves therefore requires attention to the land and water above them as well as to the formations inside.",
