    "id": "original-15",
    "title": "The Great Barrier Reef",
    "year": "Original Practice",
    "transcript": "The Great Barrier Reef lies off the northeastern coast of Australia. It extends along the coast of Queensland. The reef system stretches for roughly 2,300 kilometers. The reef is not one continuous wall of coral but an extensive collection of reefs, islands and other marine habitats. Its location along northeastern Australia places much of it within warm tropical waters. The continental shelf provides areas where sunlight can reach the seabed at relatively shallow depths. Ocean currents move water, nutrients and living organisms between different parts of the system. Conditions vary considerably from sheltered coastal locations to reefs exposed to open ocean waves. This variety helps support a wide range of marine life.\n\nReef-building corals are animals made up of small polyps. Many reef-building corals secrete calcium carbonate skeletons. Microscopic algae living in coral tissues provide much of the coral's energy. A coral polyp is a small animal with a mouth surrounded by tentacles. Many reef-building species live in colonies composed of numerous connected polyps. Their skeletons remain after individual animals die and provide surfaces on which later generations can grow. Over long periods, this accumulation contributes to the development of large reef structures. The algae living inside many corals exchange useful products with their animal hosts. This relationship helps explain how reefs can grow in waters that contain relatively low concentrations of dissolved nutrients.\n\nThe algae use sunlight through photosynthesis. Many reef-building corals thrive in clear, shallow tropical water. Coral bleaching occurs when stressed corals lose much of their symbiotic algae or pigments. Photosynthesis allows the algae to use light energy to produce organic compounds. Some of these compounds are transferred to the coral and contribute to its energy supply. Corals can also capture small organisms from the surrounding water using their tentacles. Clear water allows more light to reach the algae than water clouded by suspended sediment. When environmental stress disrupts the partnership, the coral may lose its normal coloration. The pale skeleton becomes more visible through the animal's relatively transparent tissue.\n\nUnusually warm water can trigger bleaching. Bleached corals are not necessarily dead and may recover if stress decreases. Crown-of-thorns starfish feed on living coral tissue. Prolonged exposure to unusually warm water can place severe stress on the relationship between coral and algae. A bleached colony may survive if favorable conditions return before its energy reserves are exhausted. Repeated or prolonged stress increases the risk of disease and death. Crown-of-thorns starfish are natural members of reef ecosystems, but large outbreaks can cause extensive coral loss. Recovery depends partly on the survival of nearby colonies that can produce new young corals. Different species may respond differently to the same environmental disturbance.\n\nReef fish use coral structures for shelter and feeding. Seagrass beds in the wider reef region provide food for dugongs. Runoff from land can carry sediment and nutrients into reef waters. The complex shapes of coral colonies create hiding places for small fish and other animals. Some fish graze on algae that might otherwise compete with corals for space. Seagrass meadows are distinct from coral reefs but contribute to the health of the wider marine environment. Dugongs feed mainly on seagrasses rather than on the hard skeletons of reef-building corals. Sediment carried from land can reduce the amount of sunlight reaching underwater plants and algae. Excess nutrients may also change the balance among organisms living in reef waters.\n\nMarine protected areas help manage activities that affect reef habitats. The reef was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 1981. The reef supports tourism, biodiversity and cultural connections for Traditional Owners. Management zones can limit certain activities in sensitive locations while allowing others in designated areas. Monitoring programs help researchers track changes in coral cover and the abundance of important species. Tourism provides economic opportunities but also requires practices that avoid damaging living coral. Traditional Owners have longstanding cultural relationships with the waters and islands of the region. International recognition highlights the reef's significance without removing the need for local conservation work. Its future depends on both the management of nearby activities and the broader environmental conditions affecting the ocean.",
